/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Absolute zero is 0 K, about −273.15 °C.
   - This is a reference point, not a calculation trick.
   - The law applies to a perfect crystal.
   - For reversible heating at constant pressure, dS = Cp dT / T.
   - S(T) is absolute molar entropy at T in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; T′ is the integration variable, running from 0 K up to the final temperature T in K; Cp is heat capacity at constant pressure in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; ΔHtr is the enthalpy of a phase transition in 
   - The integral cannot start at exactly 0 K in a real measurement, because Cp/T is hard to measure and 0 K is unattainable.
   - Procedure to build an absolute entropy:
   - This is why entropy tables need heat-capacity data, not just one calorimeter reading.
   - Residual entropy is entropy that remains at 0 K when a solid is not a perfect ordered crystal.
   - The third law needs the words perfect crystal.
*/
