/* book Worked example 2: Q: Build the absolute molar entropy of a substance at 298.15 K and 1 bar from these data. Below 10 K the solid follows Cp = aT³, and Cp(10 K) = 0.80 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. From 10 K to the melting point 200 K, the tabulated integral ∫Cp/T dT for the solid is 60.0 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. ΔHfus = 8.00 kJ/mol at 200 K. The liquid has Cp = 120 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ from 200 K to 298.15 K.
   1. Debye part, 0 to 10 K: S = aT³/3 = Cp(10 K)/3 = 0.80/3 = 0.27 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   2. Solid, 10 K to 200 K: 60.0 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ (given).
   3. Melting at 200 K: ΔHfus/Tm = 8000/200 = 40.0 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   4. Liquid, 200 K to 298.15 K: Cp ln(T₂/T₁) = 120 × ln(298.15/200) = 120 × 0.3993 = 47.9 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   5. Add, with S(0) = 0 for the perfect crystal: S = 0.27 + 60.0 + 40.0 + 47.9 = 148.2 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   Answer: S(298.15 K) = 148.2 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; the melting step alone gives 40.0 of it. */
