/* book exam traps: Using S = 0 at 0 °C or at 298 K. The third-law zero is 0 K, not the ice point or room temperature. | Forgetting to divide Cp by T. The entropy integral is ∫(Cp/T)dT, not ∫Cp dT. | Assuming every solid has S(0) = 0. Only a perfect ordered crystal does; CO and glasses keep residual entropy. | Omitting phase-transition terms. A complete absolute entropy adds ΔHtr/Ttr at each transition between 0 K and T. | Thinking enough cooling steps can reach exactly 0 K. The third law says 0 K is unattainable in a finite number of steps. */
